    notes: "Verified against the original 10 anchor jurisdictions (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus TX, PA, MI, WA, MA, TN, IN, MO, MD, WI, CO, MN, and SC. TX does not have a separate second-degree murder statute; it is a punishment-stage reduction within the same murder statute. PA's second degree murder IS its felony murder rule and nothing else. MI's second degree is a pure catch-all for murder that isn't first degree. WA's second degree includes a broad felony-murder path covering any felony not on first degree's enumerated list. MA's is also a pure catch-all, same statute as first degree. TN's second degree adds overdose-death provisions: a death from unlawfully distributing certain drugs is charged as second degree murder. IN, SC, and AL have no second degree murder at all; all three citations point to the same single murder statute as first degree. MO's second degree also holds its felony murder rule, applying broadly to any felony rather than an enumerated list. MD's second degree is also a pure catch-all, but unlike most states caps the sentence at 40 years rather than allowing life. WI's second degree requires the same intent-to-kill conduct as first degree; the only difference is whether the state can disprove a mitigating circumstance. CO's second degree also holds the state's felony murder rule; unusually, sudden heat of passion here lowers the felony class of second degree murder itself rather than reducing the charge to manslaughter. MN's second degree covers intentional killing without premeditation, felony murder outside the first-degree list, and an unusual path: intentional bodily harm death while subject to a protective order against the victim. Louisiana's underlying citation database had flagged its second degree murder citation 'needs_review'; that flag was independently checked during this pass and found incorrect, the statute is current and correctly cited, joining Maryland's and Alabama's earlier false-flag findings. Kentucky also has no second degree murder at all; its citation points to the same single murder statute as first degree. Oregon's second degree murder is its base homicide tier and, unusually, the one that actually contains the felony murder rule rather than first degree. Oklahoma's second degree murder covers depraved-mind reckless killing and, like Texas and Louisiana, also holds half of its own felony murder rule: a death during any felony not on first degree's enumerated list. Connecticut has no second degree murder either; its citation points to the same base murder statute as first degree. Connecticut does have a separately named, genuinely more serious tier ('murder with special circumstances,' an eight-factor list including killing a police officer or a victim under 16), but it functions as an aggravated tier on top of base murder, not a second-degree offense, so it is deliberately not mapped to this slug despite what the numbering convention elsewhere might suggest. Utah's base 'murder' tier is mapped here since, unlike Connecticut, Utah's own citation numbering runs in the expected direction; its felony-murder predicate list is unusually long at 24 separate offenses. Nevada's second degree is a pure catch-all: any unlawful killing with malice that doesn't meet one of first degree's specific enumerated paths. Iowa's second degree is also a pure catch-all, notable for an unusually high maximum among its own Class B felonies: while most Iowa Class B felonies cap at 25 years, second degree murder specifically carries up to 50. Arkansas's second degree covers extreme-indifference knowing killing, or causing death while purposely intending only serious injury rather than death itself. Mississippi's second degree uses the classic 'depraved heart' formulation: an act imminently dangerous to others, evincing a depraved heart regardless of human life, without design to kill a particular person. Kansas's second degree is a catch-all for murder that doesn't meet first degree's premeditation-or-inherently-dangerous-felony test. New Mexico's second degree is explicitly labeled a lesser included offense of first degree murder in the statute's own text, and carries a distinctive sentencing classification, 'second degree felony resulting in the death of a human being,' a more severe category than an ordinary second degree felony. Puerto Rico's second degree murder (50 years fixed) is framed as a reckless killing rather than an intent-minus-premeditation catch-all; the purposeful heat-of-passion killing that most states handle as either murder or voluntary manslaughter is instead its own separate 'asesinato atenuado' statute (15 years fixed), mapped to the manslaughter category below rather than here. Nebraska's second degree murder is a clean catch-all: an intentional killing without premeditation, Class IB felony, one tier below first degree's Class I/IA classification. Idaho's second degree murder is a pure catch-all, 'all other kinds of murder are of the second degree,' with no separate statutory definition of its own. West Virginia's second degree murder is a pure catch-all, 'all other murder,' punished by a 10-to-40-year definite term rather than first degree's fixed life sentence. Hawaii's second degree murder is a pure catch-all: intentionally or knowingly causing another's death for any circumstance not on first degree's specific list. New Hampshire's felony-murder rule is codified within second degree murder rather than first, the same structural placement Washington, Missouri, and Oregon use. Maine has no second degree murder tier; the same single 'Murder' statute cited for first degree above is the only murder offense in its code. Montana has no second degree murder tier; the same single 'deliberate homicide' statute cited for first degree above is the only murder-equivalent offense in its code. Rhode Island's second degree murder is a pure catch-all, 'any other murder,' and its own statute explicitly lets the jury find either degree or a lesser included offense even when only one degree is charged. Delaware's second degree murder carries the same Class A felony classification as first degree, unlike most states where the degree label tracks a genuinely lower penalty tier. North Dakota's second degree murder is structurally distinctive: rather than a separate, less culpable killing, it's the SAME conduct that would be Class AA (first degree) murder, mitigated specifically because it occurred under extreme emotional disturbance with a reasonable excuse, functioning as the heat-of-passion path most states instead handle as manslaughter. Vermont's second degree murder is a pure catch-all, 'all other kinds of murder,' defined in the same section as first degree. DC's second degree murder is a clean malice-aforethought catch-all, but its penalty structure is notably narrow compared to most states surveyed: up to life, generally capped at 40 years absent specific statutory circumstances, only a 10-year gap below first degree's 30-year floor. Wyoming's second degree murder is a clean malice-without-premeditation catch-all, with a notably narrow sentencing gap below first degree: not less than 20 years or during life, only a 10-year floor difference from first degree's own severe minimum. Expansion to the remaining 2 jurisdictions is ongoing.",
