    notes: "Verified against CA, FL, VA, IL, GA, IN, WI, LA, and OK. NY, OH, TX, PA, MI, WA, MA, TN, MO, MD, CO, MN, SC, and AL do not have a separate battery statute (see the assault entries). NC, NJ, and AZ also do not; their equivalents are covered under the assault-in-the-second/third-degree entries. SC combines assault and battery into a single graded statute, the same pattern as Virginia. AL folds contact-based offenses entirely into its three-tier assault scheme, closer to Ohio's approach than to South Carolina's combined-name statute. Indiana and Wisconsin are the strongest cases for this entry of any states surveyed: neither has an 'assault' crime at all, so battery is genuinely each state's core, primary offense against the person rather than one option among several. Louisiana is the clearest surviving example of the traditional common-law assault/battery split found so far: assault never requires touching and battery always does, and each side is independently graded into its own tiers (simple, second degree, and aggravated battery on the touching side); this entry covers the base simple battery tier, with the second degree and aggravated tiers covered under the assault-in-the-second/first-degree entries. Kentucky has no separate battery statute either, folding contact-based offenses entirely into its four-tier assault scheme, the same pattern as Alabama and Ohio. Oregon also has no separate battery statute, folding contact-based offenses into its four-tier assault scheme, the same pattern as Ohio, Alabama, and Kentucky. Oklahoma is the second state surveyed, after Louisiana, with genuinely separate assault and battery definitions, though the two are typically charged and referred to together as 'assault and battery' rather than as fully independent offenses. Connecticut also has no separate battery statute, folding contact-based offenses into its three-tier assault scheme. Utah also has no separate battery statute, folding contact-based offenses into its assault/aggravated assault scheme. Nevada is the third state surveyed, after Louisiana and Oklahoma, with a genuinely separate battery statute distinct from assault; battery requires no proof of actual injury, just willful unlawful force. Iowa also has no separate battery statute, but unlike Ohio/Alabama/Kentucky/Oregon/Utah's pattern of folding contact into a graded assault SCHEME, Iowa's single, ungraded assault DEFINITION itself covers both the threat and the contact within the very same subsection. Arkansas, by contrast, is the most fully built-out assault/battery split surveyed: it keeps a genuinely separate, independently numbered three-degree BATTERY ladder (all felonies, actual injury required) running alongside its separate three-degree ASSAULT ladder (all misdemeanors, no touching required) at the same time, rather than folding one concept into the other the way every other state surveyed does. Mississippi also has no separate battery statute, folding contact-based offenses into its simple/aggravated assault scheme, the same pattern as Ohio, Alabama, Kentucky, Oregon, Utah, and Connecticut. Kansas, by contrast, is the third state surveyed with a genuinely separate battery statute (touching required) alongside its separate assault statute (no touching required), the same structural pattern as Arkansas, Louisiana, Oklahoma, and Nevada. New Mexico is the fourth: base battery is simply unlawful, intentional touching done in a rude, insolent, or angry manner, with no injury requirement at all. Puerto Rico has no separate battery statute; 'agresión' (Art. 122) is itself the unwanted-physical-contact offense, the same statute cited for assault in the third degree above. This mirrors the unified assault/battery structure found in Indiana, Wisconsin, and Iowa rather than the split structure found in Louisiana, Oklahoma, Nevada, Arkansas, Kansas, and New Mexico. Nebraska has no separate battery statute; base 'assault in the third degree' is itself the unwanted-physical-contact offense, the same statute cited for assault in the third degree above, the same unified structure found in Indiana, Wisconsin, Iowa, and Puerto Rico. Idaho's base battery statute, any willful and unlawful use of force or violence on another person, is the same statute mapped to assault in the third degree above, reflecting Idaho's genuine assault/battery split rather than a separate battery-specific entry. West Virginia's base battery statute, unlawful and intentional insulting/provoking contact or physical harm, is the contact-based counterpart to the third degree assault entry above, reflecting the state's genuine common-law assault/battery split. Hawaii has no separate battery statute; base assault in the third degree is itself the unwanted-physical-contact offense, mirroring the unified assault/battery structure found in Indiana, Wisconsin, Iowa, Puerto Rico, and Nebraska. New Hampshire's domestic violence statute is popularly known as 'Joshua's Law,' the first state surveyed where the statute itself carries a widely used personal-name nickname; it uses a clean binary structure, misdemeanor base escalating straight to a felony if a deadly weapon is used or threatened, with no intermediate tiers. Maine has no separate battery statute; base assault, which already covers bodily injury or offensive physical contact, is the same statute cited for assault in the third degree above. Montana has no separate battery statute; base assault, which already covers insulting/provoking physical contact, is the same statute cited for assault in the third degree above. Rhode Island combines assault and battery terminology into single statutes at both the misdemeanor and felony level; this entry cites the same base statute as assault in the third degree above. Delaware has no separate battery statute; base assault in the third degree, which already covers intentional or reckless physical injury, is the same statute cited for assault in the third degree above. North Dakota's domestic violence statute mirrors the general assault ladder's three injury-severity tiers applied to a family/household member, with automatic felony escalation when the victim is under 12 and a genuine second-offense-across-any-assault-statute escalation trigger. Vermont's domestic assault statute is unusually flat: a single penalty tier applies regardless of whether the conduct was an attempt, actual bodily injury, or merely causing fear of imminent serious injury, unlike the general assault ladder's graded severity tiers. DC has no separate battery statute; base 'assault in the third degree,' unlawfully assaulting or threatening another in a menacing manner, is itself the unwanted-contact offense, the same unified structure found in Nebraska, Indiana, Wisconsin, and Puerto Rico. Wyoming keeps a genuine, separately defined battery offense distinct from simple assault: actual bodily injury caused by physical force, as opposed to simple assault's mere attempt, with a related 'unlawful contact' provision covering rude or insolent touching at the same misdemeanor penalty level. Remaining 2 jurisdictions not yet checked.",
