    degreeContext: "California defines robbery as the felonious taking of personal property from someone's person or immediate presence, against their will, by force or fear (Penal Code § 211), then grades it into first or second degree based on the setting and circumstances. Florida's degree line depends specifically on whether the offender carried a firearm or other weapon: carrying one makes it first degree, not carrying one makes it second degree. New York defines robbery as forcible stealing, using or threatening immediate force during a larceny to take or keep property. Virginia's statute does not define robbery in the text itself; it sets penalties by circumstance instead, ranging from a Class 6 felony for robbery by threat with no weapon up to a Class 2 felony when the robbery causes serious injury or death. Ohio's base robbery statute covers having a deadly weapon, inflicting or threatening physical harm, or using or threatening force during a theft, and is a second or third degree felony depending on which of those applies; a separate 'aggravated robbery' statute covers more serious circumstances. Illinois' base robbery is force or threatened force during a taking, a Class 2 felony (Class 1 if the victim is 60 or older, disabled, or the crime happens at a school, day care, or place of worship); Illinois' armed robbery enhancement applies even if the offender only indicates having a weapon without actually possessing one. Georgia's robbery statute explicitly includes 'sudden snatching,' meaning a purse-snatch-style taking can qualify without separate proof of force or fear. North Carolina only has a codified robbery statute for armed robbery (robbery with a dangerous weapon); unarmed robbery is prosecuted under judge-made common law rather than a specific statute. New Jersey's robbery is a second degree crime by default, elevated to first degree for an attempted killing, serious injury, or a weapon. Arizona's base robbery is a class 4 felony for taking property using or threatening force, with a separate, more serious armed robbery statute for weapon use.",
