    degreeContext: "California and Florida do not require breaking in. California's burglary statute covers entering any house, room, or other listed structure with intent to commit theft or any felony inside. Florida's covers entering a dwelling, structure, or vehicle with intent to commit an offense inside, and grades the offense by degree based on whether the offender assaulted someone, was armed, caused significant property damage, or entered an occupied versus unoccupied space. New York requires knowingly entering or remaining unlawfully in a building with intent to commit a crime, with first degree adding aggravating factors like a weapon, injury to someone present, or displaying what appears to be a firearm. Virginia's core burglary statute is unusually traditional: it requires an actual breaking and entering of a dwelling specifically at night, a Class 3 felony, elevated to Class 2 if the person was armed; other Virginia statutes cover daytime or non-dwelling entry with different penalties. Ohio's most serious version, aggravated burglary, requires trespassing by force, stealth, or deception into an occupied structure with someone other than an accomplice present, combined with either inflicting or threatening physical harm or possessing a deadly weapon. Illinois, like California, needs no breaking, just knowingly entering or remaining without authority with intent to commit a felony or theft, and separately criminalizes residential burglary of a dwelling as a more serious offense. Georgia grades by whether the structure is a dwelling: first degree covers any dwelling, occupied or not, even one that is vacant, while second degree covers non-dwelling buildings and structures. North Carolina still requires an actual breaking and entering into a dwelling or sleeping apartment, like Virginia, but unlike Virginia has no nighttime requirement; degree there turns on whether the dwelling was occupied at the time. New Jersey's degree split is based on harm and weapons rather than the type of structure: it is second degree if the person inflicts, attempts, or threatens injury, or is armed with or displays what appears to be a weapon or explosives, and third degree otherwise. Arizona layers a weapon-based first degree on top of an underlying residential/non-residential distinction: entering a structure with intent to commit a felony or theft is third degree (nonresidential) or second degree (residential), and either becomes first degree if the person knowingly possesses explosives, a deadly weapon, or a dangerous instrument while committing it.",
