    degreeContext: "The felony threshold varies dramatically by state. New Jersey's is by far the lowest found so far: theft of $200 or more is already a fourth degree crime (New Jersey's felony-equivalent tier). Illinois sets its threshold at $500, Florida at $750, and New York, Virginia, Ohio, and Arizona all at $1,000. North Carolina's is also $1,000, though certain items (property taken from a person, firearms, explosives, and repeat offenders with four or more prior larceny convictions) are automatic felonies regardless of value. Georgia's is the highest surveyed at $1,500. Above these baselines, states structure their felony tiers very differently: Florida has three grand theft degrees topping out at $100,000+, Ohio has five felony degrees scaling to $1,500,000+, and Illinois has felony tiers reaching a non-probationable Class 1 felony and Class X felony at the highest amounts. California's theft statute (Penal Code § 484) does not state a specific felony dollar threshold in the base definition itself; that threshold is set in a separate section. Certain items, including firearms and motor vehicles, are often treated as felony-level theft in some states regardless of their dollar value.",
