    degreeContext: "California, Florida, and Virginia all use 0.08% BAC as the standard per se threshold, alongside a separate general-impairment test that does not depend on a specific number. New York adds a lower tier: Driving While Ability Impaired (DWAI) applies below the 0.08% DWI threshold, and there is a separate aggravated DWI at 0.18% BAC, with an additional enhancement if a child under 16 is in the car. Ohio's OVI statute does not use a simple single threshold: the standard offense covers 0.08% up to 0.16% BAC, with a separate 'high-test' enhanced penalty starting at 0.17%. Illinois, Georgia, North Carolina, and Arizona all add a zero-tolerance rule for controlled substances: any detectable amount of certain drugs or their metabolites in your system while driving is a violation on its own, with no impairment or specific concentration required. Arizona's impairment standard is unusually strict, worded as impaired 'to the slightest degree.' New Jersey is unusual in a different way: DWI is codified in its motor vehicle title rather than its criminal code, and a first offense has three separate BAC tiers (0.08 to 0.10, 0.10 or more, and 0.15 or more) with penalties that increase directly with the BAC level, not just with prior offenses. First offense DUI is usually a misdemeanor (or a non-criminal traffic violation in New Jersey) across these states, but a high BAC, prior offenses, an accident causing injury, or a child passenger can elevate the charge or penalty significantly.",
