    notes: "California calls this offense 'vandalism' and sets its felony/misdemeanor line at $400 in damage, notably lower than most states' $500-$1,000 threshold, with a $50,000 fine ceiling once damage reaches $10,000 or more. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.New York's base criminal mischief tier sets its reckless-damage threshold at $250 and includes a distinctive standalone path for disabling emergency communication equipment to prevent someone from calling for help, unrelated to any property-value threshold. Florida grades primarily by dollar damage across three clean tiers, with automatic third degree felony treatment for a prior conviction or for damaging religious property. Virginia splits first on intent to steal rather than value: damage without intent to steal is a flat Class 3 misdemeanor dismissible if the owner confirms full restitution by affidavit, while intentional damage is graded separately by value. Ohio's actual 'criminal mischief' statute (§2909.07) is narrowly limited to tampering with specific systems (survey markers, safety devices, computers); the real general-vandalism equivalent is criminal damaging (§2909.06), which uses NO dollar-value threshold at all, grading instead purely by risk of harm to a person. Illinois doubles its entire value ladder up one full class at every tier for damage to schools, places of worship, farm equipment, or memorials, rather than adding a single flat enhancement. Georgia has no misdemeanor tier at all under this section: intentional damage over $500, or ANY fire/explosive damage regardless of value, is a flat 1-to-5-year felony. North Carolina is the opposite extreme: a simple two-tier misdemeanor-only structure split at $200, with no felony tier for ordinary property damage under this statute at all. New Jersey escalates all the way to second degree if reckless tampering with a public service (water, power, communication) causes a death, a severe outcome-based jump layered on the dollar-value tiers. Arizona requires only RECKLESS conduct at every tier rather than intentional damage, and automatically grades any gang-related property destruction at Class 5 felony regardless of value. Texas layers independent target-based enhancements (habitations, agricultural fencing, utilities, schools, catalytic converters) on top of its value ladder. Pennsylvania grades graffiti on its own separate, lower $150 threshold rather than the $500 line used for ordinary property damage. Michigan combines dollar amount and prior convictions as independent cross-cutting triggers, so a small-value repeat offense reaches the same felony tier as a much larger first offense. Washington adds a distinctive top-tier path for tampering with an official ballot deposit box, graded identically to $5,000+ in ordinary damage. Massachusetts's primary grading axis is mens rea, not dollar value: willful and malicious destruction reaches 10 years while merely wanton destruction has no state-prison exposure at all. Tennessee grades ordinary vandalism as theft by value, but carves out a distinctive lower flat misdemeanor tier specifically for government property under $2,500. Indiana runs a parallel two-track structure, ordinary criminal mischief alongside a separate 'institutional criminal mischief' provision for religious, school, and research-facility targets with its own value ladder. Missouri calls this 'property damage' and automatically bumps the degree specifically when the victim is a law enforcement officer or their close relative. Maryland is a genuine outlier: this offense stays a misdemeanor at every tier, with no felony escalation at all regardless of how much damage occurred. Wisconsin has distinct named felony enhancements for rock art sites, research plants, vending machines, and energy infrastructure, an unusually specific breadth of named targets. Colorado uses the exact same eight-tier value ladder for criminal mischief as its general theft statute, an unusually direct alignment. Minnesota builds bias motivation directly into this statute's own degree structure, with its own dedicated second and third degree tiers for bias-motivated damage. South Carolina separately enhances damage to places of worship to a 6-month-to-10-year felony floor regardless of dollar value. Alabama layers a mandatory-minimum jail term onto repeat religious-property convictions within 5 years, without changing the misdemeanor classification itself. Louisiana's top tier requires a genuinely severe mandatory minimum of 1 year at hard labor once damage crosses $50,000. Kentucky builds a pre-trial restorative off-ramp directly into the statute, repair or restitution can reduce the felony down to a misdemeanor before trial. Oregon's lowest tier requires no damage threshold at all, merely tampering with intent to cause substantial inconvenience qualifies. Connecticut's top tier separately criminalizes tampering with a court-ordered electronic monitoring device at felony level. Utah reaches second-degree felony status for critical infrastructure disruption regardless of the dollar value of any damage. Nevada's statute applies only when no other specific law covers the conduct, grading damage proportionately without fixed dollar brackets in its own text. Iowa reaches fourth degree for damaging a land-surveyor's boundary monument at any dollar value at all. Arkansas reaches its top felony tier at any damage amount when critical infrastructure is involved. Mississippi grades malicious mischief on the identical value ladder used for its own grand larceny and forgery statutes. Kansas reaches a harsher aggravated tier for metal-theft-motivated vandalism over $5,000, regardless of the base statute's own value ladder. Nebraska has a standalone felony tier for disrupting rail infrastructure or utilities, regardless of the resulting dollar loss. Idaho's felony threshold sits at exactly $1,000, and the statute explicitly protects co-owned marital property from this charge. West Virginia has a distinctive low-penalty carve-out for destroying boundary monuments or posted no-trespassing signs. Hawaii independently reaches felony status for damage during a declared emergency, regardless of the dollar amount. New Hampshire independently criminalizes disabling a smoke detector in a rental dwelling, unrelated to dollar value. Maine has no monetary threshold at all, a single flat class, but separately targets logging-equipment sabotage specifically. Montana independently criminalizes failing to close a gate on enclosed rural premises as its own listed conduct. Rhode Island has no value tiers at all, but mandates 100 hours of community service on a first offense, 200 on repeat. Delaware's criminal mischief statute independently reaches felony status for interrupting public utility service, regardless of the dollar value of damage. North Dakota's criminal mischief statute independently reaches felony status for damage caused by an explosive or destructive device, regardless of dollar value. Vermont's own literal statute name is 'unlawful mischief,' grading damage by an explosive device at the same top felony tier as damage over $1,000 regardless of the device's own dollar impact. DC's criminal mischief statute explicitly reaches destruction 'by fire or otherwise,' overlapping with its arson statute for lower-value property damage. Wyoming grades property destruction by restoration cost, but makes it a felony regardless of value if the target is unlawfully entered agricultural or grazing land. Alaska reserves its first degree tier for a narrow, infrastructure-focused set of paths like damaging an oil or gas pipeline rather than a simple top-dollar-value threshold. New Mexico uses an unusually simple two-tier structure, a petty misdemeanor up to $1,000 in damage and a single felony tier above that. Puerto Rico's aggravated tier turns on five independent triggers, including damage to historically or culturally significant property, rather than dollar value alone.",
