    notes: "Created by Proposition 47 as its own standalone misdemeanor for entering a business to steal $950 or less; California expressly bars charging the same conduct as burglary or theft instead, closing off a route around Prop 47's misdemeanor treatment. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.New York has no standalone shoplifting statute; a shoplifting-type theft is simply charged as ordinary petit or grand larceny, the same statute used for any other theft. Florida's retail theft statute targets organized and repeat shoplifting specifically, coordinated rings, resale conspiracies, or 10+ items across stores in 120 days, rather than a single low-value taking. Virginia's shoplifting statute is a simple concealment-or-price-tampering offense graded by the general larceny value line, with concealment on store premises itself creating a presumption of intent to steal. Ohio has no standalone shoplifting statute at all; a shoplifting-type taking is charged as ordinary theft under the general value ladder. Illinois's retail theft statute lists eight distinct prohibited acts beyond simple concealment, including under-ringing at a register and failing to return leased property after a 10-day demand. Georgia uses its own dedicated $500 line with a graduated misdemeanor repeat-offense ladder (mandatory fine, then mandatory jail days) that still stays below felony level. North Carolina's repeat-offense ladder culminates in an 11-day MANDATORY minimum on a third offense that good-time credit cannot reduce, and separately makes security-defeating devices an automatic felony regardless of value. New Jersey adds an organized-retail-theft path with its own much lower $1,000 threshold for its top two felony degrees, and mandates minimum community service or jail time baked directly into the statute. Arizona automatically bumps any shoplifting with a prior theft conviction to Class 4 felony regardless of the current incident's value, a categorical rather than value-scaled repeat-offense rule. Texas has no standalone shoplifting statute at all; it's prosecuted as ordinary theft under the general value ladder. Pennsylvania uses a combined prior-offense-and-value matrix where a second offense under $150 is already a misdemeanor and a third offense is an automatic felony regardless of value. Michigan has a dedicated retail fraud statute with a genuinely high $1,000 line for its top felony tier, and automatically bumps repeat offenders to first-degree regardless of current value. Washington does not appear to have a standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the same consolidated theft law. Massachusetts uses a graduated fine-only ladder by offense count below $250, with jail only becoming available on a third offense. Tennessee has no standalone shoplifting statute at all; it's prosecuted under the same general theft value ladder. Indiana has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the same general theft value ladder. Missouri's stealing statute carries a distinctive enhanced-penalty provision specifically for organized, coordinated retail theft exceeding $750. Maryland has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the same general theft value ladder. Wisconsin extends this statute to theft of SERVICES (absconding without paying a service provider), and bumps a sub-$500 theft to felony when tied to an internet-resale conspiracy. Colorado has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general theft value ladder. Minnesota likewise has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the same consolidated theft law. South Carolina, unlike most states surveyed, has a dedicated standalone shoplifting statute separate from its general larceny law. Alabama has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the same general theft framework used for financial-fraud. Louisiana has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general theft law, which itself names retail-specific behaviors as evidence. Kentucky likewise has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general theft-by-unlawful-taking framework. Oregon has a distinctive separate organized-retail-theft felony for coordinated rings stealing $5,000+ within 180 days. Connecticut explicitly names shoplifting as one of its 18 listed larceny conduct categories rather than a standalone offense. Utah, unlike most states surveyed, has a dedicated standalone retail theft statute with its own catalytic-converter automatic-felony trigger. Nevada has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general theft value ladder. Iowa has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general five-degree theft ladder. Arkansas likewise has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general theft value ladder. Mississippi has a dedicated shoplifting statute where a third conviction for $500-$1,000 in merchandise independently becomes a felony. Kansas has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general theft statute's value ladder. Nebraska has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general theft statute. Idaho has a dedicated shoplifting statute framed entirely around concealment, with no dollar-value threshold at all. West Virginia requires a mandatory restitution payment to the store, $50 or double the merchandise value, on every conviction. Hawaii has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's explicitly named as one conduct category within the general theft statute. New Hampshire has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the consolidated theft statute. Maine likewise has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general theft statute. Montana has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general theft value ladder. Rhode Island likewise has no standalone shoplifting statute; it's prosecuted under the general larceny value ladder. Delaware's shoplifting statute reaches felony status through a multi-store aggregation path: $1,500 taken across 3+ stores counts the same as a single $1,500 theft. North Dakota gives shoplifting its own more lenient first-offense misdemeanor tier than general theft, but a fourth offense within four years jumps straight to felony. Vermont has a genuine standalone shoplifting statute rather than folding retail theft into its general larceny value ladder. DC has no standalone shoplifting statute; retail theft is prosecuted under the same general theft statute, including its distinctive six-month aggregation rule. Wyoming has no standalone shoplifting statute at all, prosecuting retail theft under the general theft law's ordinary value-based grading. Alaska's equivalent, titled 'concealment of merchandise,' treats merchandise found concealed on a person as prima facie evidence of knowing concealment. New Mexico grades shoplifting across five value tiers with a distinctive 'aggravated shoplifting' path for using a deadly weapon to retain the goods or escape. Puerto Rico's version escalates from a first-offense misdemeanor to a flat fixed 3-year felony specifically on a second or subsequent conviction for this same offense, regardless of the merchandise's value.",
