    notes: "California's disorderly conduct statute is a genuinely broad omnibus provision folding in lewd conduct, prostitution/solicitation, begging, loitering near restrooms, public intoxication (with a mandatory 72-hour civil hold rather than jail), and voyeurism/unlawful recording, all under one statute. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.Like trespass, New York classifies disorderly conduct as a VIOLATION rather than a misdemeanor, its lightest classification tier, covering fighting, unreasonable noise, obstructing traffic, and refusing a dispersal order among other paths. Florida uses old-fashioned, broadly worded 'corrupts the public morals' language rather than an enumerated list of prohibited acts. Virginia's statute exempts elementary and secondary students from prosecution for conduct at school or school events, a carve-out not seen in most other states' disorderly conduct laws. Ohio's base tier is a minor misdemeanor, the lightest classification in the entire state offense structure, one notch below even a fourth degree misdemeanor. Illinois builds its disorderly conduct statute almost entirely around false reports (fire alarms, bomb threats, 911 calls, child-abuse reports) rather than the fighting/noise/public-alarm test most other states use as the base offense. Georgia includes a distinctive standalone path criminalizing obscene or profane language directed at a child under 14, in person or by phone. North Carolina grades funeral/memorial-service disruption on its own steep separate ladder, escalating from misdemeanor to Class H felony by offense count, far harsher than the flat misdemeanor covering every other disorderly-conduct path. New Jersey's statute is a single flat petty disorderly persons offense, the state's lowest criminal classification, with an unusually broad definition of 'public' expressly including apartment buildings and neighborhoods. Arizona makes reckless display or discharge of a deadly weapon during disorderly conduct its own standalone Class 6 felony, distinct from every other form of the offense. Texas lists eleven distinct prohibited acts including creating noxious odors by chemical means and several firearm-specific paths folded into the same statute. Pennsylvania's felony-adjacent misdemeanor tier ties specifically to intent to cause substantial harm or persistence after a warning, not to any objective severity measure. Michigan's disorderly-persons statute is a sprawling omnibus that folds in window peeping, vagrancy, begging, and even baseline family nonsupport as the same misdemeanor offense. Washington adds a distinctive, specifically measured funeral-protest provision, conduct within 500 feet of a funeral or burial that adversely affects the event. Massachusetts's first offense is fine-only, capped at $150, with jail time only available on a subsequent offense. Tennessee's version is a narrow, flat Class C misdemeanor covering fighting, dispersal-order refusal, hazardous conditions, and unreasonable noise. Indiana has two specific standalone felony enhancements: disrupting airport security, and conduct within 500 feet of a funeral that adversely affects the event. Missouri grades purely by offense count, shifting to a flat $1,000-$5,000 fine on a third conviction rather than escalating toward incarceration. Maryland includes a peculiar county-specific sixth path banning overnight beach bonfires in Worcester County alone, a hyper-local provision. Wisconsin explicitly exempts lawful firearm and knife possession from this statute absent other evidence of criminal intent, a specific legislative carve-out. Colorado grades each of its five listed paths on its own separate tier, from petty offense up to Class 1 misdemeanor for firearm discharge. Minnesota's lawful-assembly-disruption element has already been held unconstitutional by a court, a notable legal-history finding. South Carolina caps this offense at an unusually low $100 fine or 30 days, among the lightest maximums surveyed in this project. Alabama expressly protects a visibly holstered, legally carried firearm from being treated as disorderly conduct on its own. Louisiana has a genuinely distinctive set of funeral-protection provisions carrying their own elevated penalty above the flat base tier. Kentucky separately criminalizes creating any hazardous or physically offensive condition serving no legitimate purpose, a broad open-ended catch-all. Oregon separately criminalizes initiating a false emergency report and elevates the penalty within 200 feet of a funeral service. Connecticut folds a peeping-tom-style covert-observation offense directly into its general disorderly conduct statute. Utah has a distinctive graduated-warning structure, starting at a mere infraction and escalating one full step per repeat violation within 5 years. Nevada's version is titled breach of the peace and is a single flat misdemeanor with no graded tiers at all. Iowa criminalizes flag desecration only when done to incite trespass or assault, a narrow incitement-based test. Arkansas separately lists public exposure and damaging patriotic or religious symbols as their own qualifying paths. Mississippi's statute is built almost entirely around refusing a specific enumerated law enforcement order during a breach-of-peace situation. Kansas is unusually narrow, built around only three paths, with fighting words defined using the precise constitutional standard. Nebraska's statute is unusually terse, a single flat misdemeanor built around one general standard rather than an enumerated list. Idaho has a standalone subsection specifically protecting funeral services from malicious disturbance. West Virginia applies only to an enumerated list of place types and requires an officer's request to stop before an offense occurs. Hawaii's identical conduct is only a non-criminal violation by default, becoming a petty misdemeanor only with intent or persistence. New Hampshire's identical conduct is only a violation by default, escalating to a misdemeanor only if it continues after a request to stop. Maine's original disorderly conduct statute was fully repealed in 2007 and replaced with this section, which separately covers funeral disruption. Montana caps most conduct at a minimal $100/10-day penalty but carves out a tenfold-harsher tier for false explosion warnings. Rhode Island folds a standalone voyeurism path, looking into a bathroom or bedroom for a lascivious purpose, into this same statute. Delaware's disorderly conduct statute folds interference with a school bus into the same unclassified-misdemeanor section as fighting and noise. North Dakota's disorderly conduct statute covers surveillance-camera window-peeping, with a built-in seven-day cure period after police notice. Vermont's disorderly conduct statute is a standalone provision separate from its harassment and stalking statutes. DC's disorderly conduct statute is unusually granular, seven separately lettered subsections covering everything from funeral disruption to nighttime noise, under one flat misdemeanor penalty. Wyoming has no statute literally titled disorderly conduct, using breach of the peace as the closest equivalent instead. Alaska's version reaches an unusually broad seventh path, intentionally exposing the buttocks or anus with reckless disregard for its offensive effect, alongside the more typical noise and fighting provisions. New Mexico's version separately criminalizes maliciously touching an occupied house to threaten its occupants, a path not seen framed this way in other states surveyed. Puerto Rico layers a distinctive two-tier mandatory fine schedule on top of its misdemeanor classification, a higher fine for committing the offense inside a government building than at a private business.",
