    notes: "Base kidnapping is 3, 5, or 8 years (5, 8, or 11 if the victim is under 14), while a separate aggravated-kidnapping statute for ransom/extortion or to facilitate robbery/a sex offense reaches life without parole or life with parole, with the robbery/sex-offense path requiring the movement to have increased the victim's risk of harm beyond what the underlying offense itself would have caused. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.New York's second degree kidnapping is a strikingly minimal base offense, simply 'abducts another person' with no further elements spelled out, while first degree requires ransom intent, 12+ hour restraint with an aggravating intent, or the victim's death. Florida already sets its base kidnapping at a first degree felony up to life, escalating to a life felony only for a child-under-13 confinement combined with a listed accompanying offense. Virginia calls this offense 'abduction' and separately criminalizes forced labor, with 'intimidation' in that context expanded to include threatening someone's immigration status. Ohio has a genuinely unusual inverted incentive: releasing the victim unharmed in a safe place DROPS the offense from first to second degree felony, rather than the plain aggravating-factor lists most states use. Illinois stacks eight independent aggravating paths onto aggravated kidnapping, each a minimum Class X felony, with firearm-related paths adding fixed extra years (15, 20, or 25-to-life) directly on top. Georgia's movement requirement is genuinely minimal, even slight movement can suffice, and grading turns primarily on the victim's age rather than force or injury alone. North Carolina mirrors Ohio's inverted-incentive structure: releasing the victim safely and unharmed is what triggers the LOWER second-degree tier, directly rewarding a safe release. New Jersey's aggravated tier (victim under 16 plus a sexual offense during the kidnapping) carries a mandatory 25 years without parole, one of the harshest floors surveyed for this category. Arizona uses a distinctive three-tier release structure (unharmed release, state-agreement release, or none) rather than the simpler released-or-not split most other states use. Texas's base kidnapping statute is genuinely terse, simply 'abducts another person,' with the detailed purpose-based paths reserved for a separate aggravated-kidnapping statute. Pennsylvania's base kidnapping offense is already a first degree felony with no lower base tier at all, the harshest starting point among states surveyed. Michigan requires no minimum restraint duration at all, and a single flat tier reaches life imprisonment or any term of years. Washington's extreme-mental-distress intent path notably extends to a THIRD PARTY, not just the direct victim, a broader reach than most other states' equivalent lists. Massachusetts layers independent aggravators (extortion, firearm, serious injury) that compound rather than replace a single degree number, reaching a mandatory minimum 25 years at the top. Tennessee's base kidnapping statute is genuinely minimal, defined by cross-reference to false imprisonment plus a bodily-injury-risk element, with no purpose requirement in this section at all. Indiana's most severe tier is reserved for the purpose of the abduction (ransom, hijacking, hostage) regardless of whether any physical injury actually occurred. Missouri's structure is genuinely inverted: ransom and hostage-taking sit at the TOP tier while facilitating another felony or terrorizing the victim sits at a lower tier. Maryland's base statute is unusually narrow, requiring intent to CONCEAL the person, and explicitly exempts a parent carrying their own minor child. Wisconsin's ransom-kidnapping felony automatically reduces back to the base tier if the victim is released unharmed before the first trial witness is sworn, a court-proceeding-tied deadline. Colorado's core test requires the movement to actually INCREASE THE RISK OF HARM beyond what remaining in place would have posed, a distinctive articulated risk standard. Minnesota's victim-under-16 status alone independently triggers the harshest 40-year tier, even absent any injury or purpose like ransom. South Carolina's official code page truncates before reaching this section; the citation is directional and not independently source-verified this pass. Alabama's voluntary-release affirmative defense applies only to the first-degree, purpose-driven charge, not to general second-degree abduction. Louisiana reaches its felony tier through six alternative triggers, including detention lasting 72+ hours alone, with no weapon or ransom purpose required. Kentucky can reach capital-offense status if the victim isn't released alive, placing kidnapping on the same extreme tier as murder in that outcome. Oregon's second-degree tier requires no purpose-based element at all, just non-consensual confinement with intent to interfere with liberty. Connecticut's statute is strikingly bare-bones, simply 'abducts another person,' paired with a mandatory non-suspendable 3-year minimum. Utah has a distinctive separate path: detaining a minor without a parent or guardian's consent qualifies regardless of force, injury, or duration. Nevada treats several alternative purposes as one undifferentiated felony rather than splitting them into further degrees. Iowa reaches its felony tier through ransom, a dangerous weapon, OR the victim simply being under 18, any one alone sufficing. Arkansas's base tier is Class Y felony, its most severe classification, dropping to Class B only with proven voluntary safe release. Mississippi doesn't require intent to confine at all for a vulnerable person or child under 16 seized against a guardian's will. Kansas splits kidnapping and aggravated kidnapping by a single factor, whether bodily harm was actually inflicted. Nebraska courts treat this as one offense with two penalty ranges, with the safe-release factor decided by the judge, not the jury. Idaho's first-degree tier is genuinely capital-eligible, one of the only kidnapping statutes surveyed carrying potential death. West Virginia's default penalty is life without parole, one of the most severe base-tier kidnapping penalties found in this project. Hawaii explicitly covers unlawfully obtaining labor or services through restraint, regardless of any debt-collection purpose. New Hampshire has a standalone child-specific path requiring no ransom, hostage, or terrorizing purpose at all. Maine has a genuine reckless path to kidnapping, exposing someone to injury risk qualifies even absent any listed intentional purpose. Montana's base tier requires no ransom, hostage, or terrorizing purpose at all, just isolation or force. Rhode Island is a single flat felony tier with no degree split at all, regardless of purpose. Delaware's base kidnapping tier already includes a built-in safe-release mitigation directly in the offense definition. North Dakota's kidnapping statute drops a full grade, from class A to class B felony, for the same voluntary safe-release mitigation. Vermont's kidnapping statute has the same safe-release mitigation seen in several other states, capping the sentence at 30 years if the victim is voluntarily released unharmed before arraignment. DC's kidnapping statute is a single flat felony tier with no degree split and no safe-release mitigation built in at all. Wyoming grades its kidnapping statute entirely by whether the victim is voluntarily released substantially unharmed in a safe place before trial. Alaska's statute includes a distinct path for restraining someone to facilitate a sexual offense against a minor specifically. New Mexico's safe-release mitigation still leaves the offense a felony rather than dropping it further, a second degree felony rather than the base first degree. Puerto Rico's kidnapping statute explicitly does not require the victim's movement to cover a substantial time or distance, mere movement from one place to another is enough to consummate the offense.",
