    notes: "A clean four-tier felony ladder graded by outcome and target (great bodily injury, inhabited structure, structure/forest land, property generally) rather than by intent variations; burning your own personal property is generally not arson absent fraud or injury to another. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.New York's base arson tier is unusually reckless-only rather than intentional (recklessly damaging property by intentionally starting a fire), while the top tier requires an incendiary device or explosive plus a known or reasonably foreseeable non-participant present in the structure. Florida uses a two-tier occupancy-based structure (first degree for occupied/dwelling, second degree for other structures) rather than California's four-tier outcome ladder. Virginia's occupied-structure arson carries one of the harshest floors surveyed, life or a minimum of five years, dropping all the way to a Class 4 felony for the identical conduct against an unoccupied structure. Ohio splits this offense across two entirely separate statutes by risk to people rather than degree numbers within one section, with aggravated arson reaching first degree felony for mere risk creation, regardless of dollar value. Illinois uses a clean two-tier occupancy structure where damaging a dwelling or place of worship jumps straight to Class 1 felony regardless of value, and extends coverage to co-owned property even where the offender holds a partial ownership stake. Georgia's first-degree arson statute reaches five entirely distinct paths under one section, including defrauding an insurer or a spouse/co-owner's rights, all graded identically. North Carolina uses a clean single-factor occupancy test, whether the dwelling was occupied at the time of burning, to split first from second degree. New Jersey inverts the usual severity order: its single highest tier is reserved for 'fire-for-hire' (being paid to set a fire) rather than for the most physically destructive arson itself. Arizona's occupied-structure arson statute is genuinely terse, a flat Class 2 felony with no aggravating circumstances or insurance-fraud paths spelled out within the section itself. Texas's base felony structure covers reckless endangerment or targeting another's inhabited building, with a state-jail-felony carve-out specifically for arson during drug manufacturing. Pennsylvania separately protects buildings simply for being historic (100+ years old or register-listed) regardless of occupancy or value, its own distinct middle tier. Michigan covers any multiunit dwelling regardless of actual occupancy at the same flat life-or-any-term-of-years tier as arson causing injury. Washington treats $10,000+ insurance-fraud arson as equally severe as arson endangering human life, rather than a lesser category. Massachusetts uses a single flat felony tier up to 20 years regardless of ownership or occupancy, with dwelling defined broadly to include hotels, dormitories, and hospitals. Tennessee's base arson offense explicitly extends to farm equipment as an equally covered target, a distinctive agricultural-economy inclusion. Indiana carves out a distinctive low-value tier for $250-$5,000 in damage at a much lower felony level, with fraud-motivated arson graded there regardless of value. Missouri folds a distinctive drug-production-specific path directly into its base arson statute: starting a fire while attempting to manufacture methamphetamine. Maryland's flat felony tier reaches a genuinely steep $50,000 fine ceiling, one of the higher fine caps for this category surveyed. Wisconsin uses a single flat Class C felony tier across property damage, insurance fraud, and explosives, with no occupancy-based escalation within this section at all. Colorado uses a clean, purely occupancy-based two-tier split, one of the more minimal arson structures surveyed. Minnesota's three arson paths share an identical 20-year prison ceiling, with only the FINE amount varying by path. South Carolina's first-degree tier sets a 30-year mandatory minimum when death or serious injury results, a floor rather than a ceiling. Alabama reaches its top felony tier even without actual knowledge of anyone's presence, so long as their presence was a reasonable possibility. Louisiana's top tier turns on foreseeable danger to human life rather than actual knowledge anyone was present, with a mandatory 2-year no-parole floor. Kentucky specifically covers insurance-fraud arson under the identical felony class as burning someone else's property outright. Oregon's degree split turns on property type and danger created rather than dollar value alone. Connecticut's base tier requires intentional ignition but only reckless causation of the resulting damage, a distinctive split mens rea. Utah's insurance-fraud arson path reaches felony status at any damage amount, no dollar threshold at all, unlike its ordinary value-graded tiers. Nevada's first-degree tier applies whether or not the structure was occupied, and even covers burning one's own property. Iowa's second-degree tier is defined purely as a residual category, everything that isn't first degree. Arkansas independently reaches a severe felony tier for agricultural or timber arson over $5,000, overriding its own value ladder. Mississippi imposes treble civil damages directly within its criminal arson statute, layered on top of the criminal sentence. Kansas separately charges an accidental fire during illegal drug manufacturing as arson, even without intent to burn anything. Nebraska folds a felony-murder-style predicate structure directly into arson for fires started during robbery or burglary. Idaho enumerates an unusually detailed list of structures presumed occupied, from jails to department stores. West Virginia's first-degree tier is defined by dwelling status, including hospitals, nursing homes, and jails as qualifying dwellings. Hawaii's arson lives in its own dedicated code part, reached through either endangerment or a $1,500 damage threshold independently. New Hampshire reserves its top felony tier for historic structures, treated identically to an occupied structure. Maine's arson is a single flat top-tier felony at every path, with no lesser-degree structure built into the statute at all. Montana requires no minimum value for structures or vehicles, but $1,500 for unowned personal property specifically. Rhode Island's mandatory floor jumps to 20 years if the fire causes a death, including a responding firefighter's. Delaware's top arson tier turns on the actor's knowledge that someone was or could reasonably be present, not on actual injury. North Dakota's arson statute unusually covers an actor's own property; burning it for insurance or to defeat a co-owner's interest is the same felony as burning someone else's building. Vermont's top arson tier covers dwellings and adjoining structures regardless of occupancy status, and applies even when the property burned is the actor's own. DC's arson statute carries a genuine mandatory 1-year floor alongside its 10-year ceiling, and specifically names churches, schools, and government buildings. Wyoming's four-degree structure grades primarily by intent and outcome rather than a single value threshold, escalating to an aggravated tier for reckless disregard causing death. Alaska's first degree tier expressly defines 'another person' to include responding firefighters and police regardless of rank. New Mexico's 'occupied structure' definition reaches boats, trailers, and airplanes used for shelter or business, not just conventional buildings. Puerto Rico's arson statute explicitly clarifies that the building need not be destroyed, merely catching fire in any part of its material is enough to consummate the offense.",
