    notes: "California's stalking statute requires BOTH a repeated harassing course of conduct AND a separate credible threat element, a higher bar than harassment alone; a restraining-order violation or qualifying prior escalates the base misdemeanor directly to a felony. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.New York's fourth-degree stalking statute expressly defines 'following' to include unauthorized GPS or electronic location tracking, and covers threats to employment through continued workplace contact after a warning to stop. Florida's aggravated stalking felony tier is triggered by a credible threat, a protective order violation, a victim under 16, or a qualifying sex-offense conviction. Virginia builds in a rebuttable presumption of intent whenever the person contacts or follows the target after being told to stop, a lower practical bar than requiring a standalone credible threat. Ohio's menacing-by-stalking statute lists nine separate aggravating facts that each independently trigger felony treatment, a notably broad escalation list. Illinois's base stalking offense is ALREADY a Class 4 felony, unlike most other states surveyed where base stalking is only a misdemeanor. Georgia's statute explicitly does NOT require an overt threat of death or bodily injury to have been made, a notably lower bar, and adds a path for publishing someone's information to cause OTHERS to harass them. North Carolina's first-offense misdemeanor carries MANDATORY supervised probation rather than it being merely available, and violating an existing court order is graded even more seriously than an ordinary repeat offense. New Jersey escalates to third degree specifically when the defendant was already serving a sentence, parole, or probation for any indictable offense, tying severity to unrelated criminal-justice status. Arizona's base stalking offense is already a Class 5 felony with no misdemeanor tier at all, a genuine outlier among states surveyed. Texas's base stalking offense is already a third degree felony with no misdemeanor tier, and explicitly extends 'property' to include pets and assistance animals. Pennsylvania escalates to felony level for a first offense too, specifically when the defendant has a prior conviction against the SAME victim for an unrelated violent crime. Michigan requires the harassment to both objectively warrant distress AND actually cause it in the specific victim, with a rebuttable presumption once contact continues after a request to stop. Washington explicitly states that prior notice is NOT required as a defense and that intent to frighten is NOT a required element for harassment specifically. Massachusetts requires both a pattern of conduct AND a separate threat element together, a higher combined bar than states treating these as alternative paths. Tennessee escalates straight to a felony if the defendant is a registered sex offender, regardless of anything else about the current conduct. Indiana's base stalking offense is already a Level 6 felony with no misdemeanor tier, escalating through seven independent aggravating factors to Level 5. Missouri's version is likewise already a felony at base and explicitly covers 'technological abuse conduct' as its own independently sufficient path. Maryland again classifies this as a misdemeanor yet allows up to 5 years, another instance of its recurring severe-misdemeanor pattern. Wisconsin's base stalking offense is already a Class I felony with no misdemeanor tier, escalating on triggers including unlawfully accessing the victim's personal records. Colorado's base stalking offense is already a Class 5 felony with no misdemeanor tier, and 'Vonnie's Law' mandates a protective order automatically upon arrest, not just conviction. Minnesota separately defines harassment, aggravated harassment, and stalking as three genuinely distinct tiers, with hate motivation listed as one of the aggravated triggers. South Carolina's official code page truncates before reaching this section; the citation is directional and not independently source-verified this pass. Alabama's first-degree tier requires both repeated harassment AND an accompanying threat of death or serious bodily harm, a combined two-part test. Louisiana mandates a psychiatric evaluation on a first conviction and bars expungement entirely at every tier. Kentucky requires both a threat AND one of four additional aggravating circumstances, a genuinely layered multi-part test. Oregon's base stalking tier is already a Class B felony with no misdemeanor tier at all. Connecticut's more severe sibling tier separately covers workplace harassment and doxxing, two modern conduct types. Utah expressly forecloses both a lack-of-notice and a lack-of-intent defense directly in the statute's text. Nevada's official statute page truncates before this section; the citation is confirmed via table of contents but full text was not independently fetched this pass. Iowa bars citations in lieu of arrest for stalking and requires an initial court appearance before any release. Arkansas defines its qualifying course of conduct with a distinctive 36-hour minimum spacing between acts. Mississippi's harshest tier triggers specifically when the perpetrator is already a registered sex offender and the victim is a minor. Kansas reaches a severity level 7 felony on a first offense when the course of conduct targets a child under 14. Nebraska's base stalking tier is already a straight Class IIIA felony with no misdemeanor path at all. Idaho reaches its felony tier through any of six alternative aggravating circumstances, an unusually long qualifying list. West Virginia independently reaches a severe 2-10 year felony for conduct creating a risk the victim will self-injure. Hawaii merges harassment and stalking into one titled offense and expressly extends to email and electronic contact. New Hampshire's felony trigger is purely a prior conviction within 7 years, with no weapon or injury factor built in at all. Maine independently reaches felony status for stalking two or more members of an identifiable group. Montana treats continued contact after a stop notice as prima facie evidence of purposeful stalking conduct. Rhode Island has no misdemeanor tier at all, a straight felony from the first offense. Delaware grades stalking on its own escalating felony ladder entirely separate from harassment, which stays capped at a misdemeanor. North Dakota treats continued contact after actual notice of unwantedness as prima facie evidence of stalking intent, and separately bars misusing 911 to harass. Vermont grades stalking on its own two-tier ladder separate from harassment, with aggravated stalking independently designated a violent act for bail purposes. DC's stalking statute reaches conduct the actor merely 'should have known' would cause fear, a lower knowledge standard than an actual-intent requirement. Wyoming's stalking statute escalates from a misdemeanor to a felony on several distinct triggers, including a repeat offense within 5 years. Alaska layers six separate aggravating triggers onto its base second-degree stalking offense to reach the felony first-degree tier. New Mexico defines its 'pattern of conduct' element as two or more acts of following, monitoring, or communicating, escalating to a felony only on a second or subsequent conviction. Puerto Rico handles stalking ('acecho') entirely through a dedicated special law outside the Código Penal, the same structural pattern used for its weapons and animal-cruelty offenses.",
