    notes: "California's animal cruelty statute is unusual among wobbler offenses: both the felony and misdemeanor versions share the identical $20,000 fine ceiling rather than the fine dropping for the lesser charge. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.New York's animal cruelty offense is a genuine structural outlier: it lives in the Agriculture and Markets Law, not the Penal Law at all, though it is still treated as a criminal misdemeanor for procedural purposes. Florida's aggravated cruelty felony carries its own mandatory minimum fine and jail time specifically for proven intentional torture, on top of the base aggravated tier. Virginia's animal cruelty statute, like New York's, lives outside the general criminal code (Title 3.2, Agriculture), and its felony tier for cruel killing applies only when a death or euthanasia is linked to a prior offense within five years. Ohio's general cruelty offense under this section is a single flat misdemeanor tier with no felony escalation built into the statute itself, unlike most states surveyed. Illinois, like New York and Virginia, keeps animal cruelty entirely outside the criminal code (in the Humane Care for Animals Act), and adds a distinctive mandatory penalty enhancement specifically when the violation occurs in front of children. Georgia's aggravated-cruelty felony tier starts at the first offense (1-5 years) and escalates sharply on a repeat aggravated conviction (1-10 years, up to $100,000). North Carolina exempts a genuinely broad list of activities (wildlife management, biomedical research, livestock production, standard breed alterations) from its cruelty statute entirely. New Jersey, like New York, Illinois, and Virginia, keeps animal cruelty outside the general criminal code entirely, in its agriculture title. Arizona lists 16 separately enumerated violation types across three tiers, with a distinctive top felony tier reserved specifically for DOMESTIC animals. Texas splits into three separate tracks by conduct type (neglect, torture, fighting) rather than one unified severity ladder. Pennsylvania singles out dogs and cats for enhanced protection above general domestic animals, with its own higher minimum fine and felony escalation on repeat offenses. Michigan is genuinely distinctive: it grades primarily by the NUMBER OF ANIMALS AFFECTED rather than the severity of harm, from a 93-day misdemeanor for one animal up to a 4-year felony for ten or more. Washington, like New York and Illinois, keeps this entirely outside the criminal code, with a separately defined sexual-abuse-of-animals provision at the same felony tier as intentional torture. Massachusetts sets an unusually severe base tier: even a first offense is already felony-level, up to 7 years, with a mandatory lifetime occupational ban from animal-related work. Tennessee uses a clean two-tier structure by offense count alone: any subsequent conviction is a felony regardless of whether the repeat conduct is more or less severe than the first. Indiana independently bumps the offense to a felony when animal abuse is used specifically to threaten a family member, a distinctive domestic-intimidation trigger. Missouri's felony tier is precisely defined as torture or mutilation 'consciously inflicted while the animal was alive,' distinguishing live torture from post-death harm. Maryland's aggravated-cruelty felony specifically and separately names sexual contact with an animal as its own qualifying act. Wisconsin escalates through five distinct steps, starting at a mere Class C forfeiture for a first violation, before reaching felony level. Colorado escalates basic cruelty to a straight felony on any second offense at all, a notably low bar for reaching felony territory. Minnesota separately criminalizes harming a service animal, escalating to its harshest 4-year tier specifically if the service animal dies. South Carolina, like several other states surveyed, keeps this offense outside its criminal code, and reserves felony status for a separately defined aggravated-cruelty tier. Alabama stays a flat misdemeanor even on a third or subsequent conviction, escalating only the mandatory-minimum fine rather than the offense classification. Louisiana's repeat-offense jump for simple cruelty reaches the exact same penalty range as its own aggravated-cruelty tier. Kentucky keeps dog fighting as its own separate felony statute, with this general neglect provision staying a flat misdemeanor with no felony escalation. Oregon runs abuse and neglect as two fully parallel tracks sharing the exact same felony-triggering circumstances. Connecticut separately criminalizes killing a police or search-and-rescue animal at up to 10 years, far harsher than its general cruelty tier. Utah grades base cruelty by mental state (intentional vs. reckless) rather than harm severity, and reserves a species-specific felony for torturing a dog or cat. Nevada has a genuinely distinctive standalone dog-fighting felony ladder graded by offense count and the participant's role. Iowa treats any prior conviction from a wide family of related animal offenses as qualifying for its felony repeat-offense tier. Arkansas's minimum fine climbs sharply by offense count while the maximum stays fixed, narrowing the range toward the ceiling. Mississippi treats intentional and merely criminally negligent conduct identically, both a flat misdemeanor with no felony tier in this section. Kansas separately lists causing a horse to lose balance or fall for sport as its own listed offense. Nebraska skips the misdemeanor tier entirely on a first offense for torture, repeated beating, or mutilation specifically. Idaho's minimum and maximum fine both climb together at each repeat-offense tier, from $100-$5,000 up to $500-$9,000. West Virginia separately names baiting an animal for amusement as its own listed misdemeanor conduct. Hawaii's felony trigger is scale-based, ten or more pet animals in one instance, rather than tied to injury severity. New Hampshire has a distinctive standalone provision protecting young horses, barring separation from their mother before 90 days old. Maine keeps this offense outside its main penal code and separately lists using an animal to intimidate a person as its own violation. Montana independently criminalizes organizing an animal race over 2 miles unless it's a sanctioned endurance event. Rhode Island keeps this outside its criminal code entirely and mandates a non-suspendable 100-hour community restitution on repeat offenses. Delaware's animal cruelty statute jumps from misdemeanor to felony specifically on intentional killing or serious injury, not on value or repeat offenses. North Dakota splits animal-welfare offenses into four separate statutes; cruelty (bone-breaking, torture) is a felony from the first offense, while abuse only escalates on a third offense. Vermont's base cruelty statute lists an unusually granular 11 separate categories of prohibited conduct in one section. DC's animal cruelty statute jumps from a $250-fine misdemeanor to a $25,000-fine felony specifically for intentional serious injury or extreme indifference to animal life. Wyoming's felony tier applies specifically to intentional or knowing torture or killing, with a separate misdemeanor path for negligent mistreatment. Alaska splits its seven paths by both act and mental state, with intentional torture reaching a felony while negligent failure to provide care stays a misdemeanor. New Mexico's 'extreme cruelty to animals' path is a felony from the first offense, while its base 'cruelty to animals' misdemeanor only escalates to a felony on a fourth conviction. Puerto Rico has no animal-cruelty provision anywhere within its 309-article Código Penal, handling this offense entirely through a dedicated animal-welfare special law instead.",
