    notes: "California has no standalone prostitution statute; prostitution and solicitation are charged under the same disorderly conduct statute (§ 647) that also covers lewd conduct and public intoxication, as one listed subsection rather than its own law. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.Unlike California, New York keeps prostitution as its own dedicated statute (Article 230) rather than folding it into a general disorderly conduct law. Florida escalates purely by repeat-offense count, from a second degree misdemeanor first offense to a third degree felony by the third. Virginia, like New York, keeps prostitution as its own dedicated statute, but requires solicitation to be followed by a substantial act in furtherance, not just the offer itself. Ohio's base tier is a third degree misdemeanor, genuinely lighter than most anchor states, but engaging in prostitution with knowledge of a positive HIV test is elevated to a felony regardless of whether transmission occurred. Illinois grants minors outright immunity from prosecution under this statute, routing them to protective custody instead, and provides an explicit affirmative trafficking defense for adults. Georgia's penalty statute shares one severe escalation ladder across prostitution and related pimping/pandering offenses, reaching 10-to-30-years and a $100,000 fine for any violation involving a minor. North Carolina offers a detailed first-offender conditional-discharge track: 12 months of structured probation resulting in full dismissal and expunction eligibility on completion, not counted as a conviction. New Jersey layers a substantial mandatory civil penalty ($10,000-$50,000) on top of the criminal sentence for every violation, funding a dedicated trafficking-survivor assistance fund. Arizona keeps a single misdemeanor classification across every offense count but escalates through a mandatory minimum jail-day ladder (15/30/60 days) baked into that same grade before converting to felony on a fourth offense. Texas is a genuine outlier: this statute currently criminalizes only the seller, not the buyer, after a 2021 amendment removed the patron-side offense from this section. Pennsylvania grades patronizing on the exact same scale as prostitution itself, and requires public disclosure of sentencing orders for repeat patronizing convictions. Michigan builds in a strong protective presumption: anyone under 18 engaged in this conduct is presumed a trafficking victim unless the prosecution proves otherwise beyond a reasonable doubt. Washington uses a single flat misdemeanor with no repeat-offense escalation ladder at all, a simpler structure than most other states surveyed. Massachusetts punishes the buyer more severely than the seller, an inversion of Texas's current buyer-decriminalized structure. Tennessee grades by location rather than repeat-offense count, with a mandatory minimum 7 days near a school specifically, using an unusually large 1.5-mile radius. Indiana's felony escalation is tied to a genuinely narrow third-offense-with-two-priors window compared to states that escalate earlier. Missouri jumps straight from a Class B misdemeanor to a Class B FELONY for known HIV-positive status at the time of the act, skipping intermediate tiers entirely. Maryland frames this specifically around PROCURING or SOLICITING prostitution, a third-party-facilitation orientation, with a strict 10-day pretrial notice deadline for the trafficking defense. Wisconsin covers five distinct conduct paths identically as a flat misdemeanor, with a discretionary consent-decree diversion path for anyone under 18. Colorado is a genuine outlier: prostitution is only a petty offense, the state's lightest classification. Minnesota grades minor-victim patronage across three distinct age bands (under 14, 14-15, 16-17) rather than one flat enhancement. South Carolina's penalty escalates sharply on repeat offenses: a third offense carries a full 1-year mandatory minimum, up from a 6-month cap on the second. Alabama's base offense is a single flat misdemeanor covering both buyers and sellers, without age-graded minor-victim tiers. Louisiana's minor-victim penalties are exceptionally severe, up to 50 years hard labor when the other party is under 14. Kentucky's base statute is a single flat misdemeanor with no repeat-offense ladder or minor-victim enhancement in this section. Oregon grades the seller and buyer identically at a flat misdemeanor with no repeat-offense ladder for either side. Connecticut's statute applies only to persons 18 or older, an age carve-out built directly into the offense definition itself. Utah has a distinctive victim-of-crime exception protecting someone who witnessed or was victimized by a serious crime and reported it in good faith. Nevada is a genuine outlier: prostitution is lawful inside a licensed house of prostitution, an explicit carve-out for its regulated-brothel counties. Iowa automatically expunges a minor seller's conviction after 2 years as a matter of law if no other convictions occurred. Arkansas layers a mandatory $1,000-$2,500 fine on buyers specifically, funding trafficking-victim services directly. Mississippi's penalty ceiling is capped at just $200 or 6 months, among the lowest maximums for this offense found in this project. Kansas titles this 'selling sexual relations' and keeps it a single flat misdemeanor with trafficking-victim status as a complete defense. Nebraska grades purely by repeat-offense count, with no felony tier at all, and mandates a substance-abuse assessment as a probation condition. Idaho retitled this offense in a 2024 update and reaches felony status only on a third or subsequent conviction. West Virginia splits operating a place of prostitution from personal participation into two genuinely distinct penalty scales. Hawaii bars sentence suspension or deferral on any subsequent offense, unusual for a petty-misdemeanor-level charge. New Hampshire imposes a mandatory $500 fine specifically on patrons, directed to the state's human trafficking task force. Maine appears to have repealed seller-side prostitution offenses in 2005 and 2021, leaving only a buyer-focused provision confirmed this pass. Montana's patron penalty (up to 5 years) is tenfold harsher than the seller's (up to 6 months) for the identical transaction. Rhode Island writes a genuinely detailed trafficking-coercion affirmative defense directly into the statute's own text. Delaware escalates its base prostitution offense specifically when committed near a school or place of worship. North Dakota keeps prostitution itself a flat misdemeanor but separately makes promoting or facilitating it a class C felony. Vermont handles the entire spectrum of prostitution-related conduct within one comprehensive nine-part statute, with a steep repeat-offense jump on a second conviction. DC grades prostitution purely by prior-offense count and grants minors full immunity from prosecution, referring them to services instead. Wyoming grades both prostitution and soliciting it as mirror-image misdemeanors, with a separate felony for promoting or compelling someone into prostitution. Alaska's statute escalates to a felony specifically when the buyer is an adult patronizing a prostitute under 18 with a specified age gap. New Mexico's base tier is an unusually light petty misdemeanor for a first offense, its lowest criminal grade, before rising to an ordinary misdemeanor on a repeat conviction. Puerto Rico covers both parties under one flat misdemeanor statute and explicitly states the sex of the parties involved is not a defense.",
