    notes: "Uses the same $950 value line as California's other property crimes (wobbler above, misdemeanor at or below absent priors); a defendant cannot be convicted of both receiving stolen property and theft for the same property, and a victim can separately sue for triple damages. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.New York calls this offense 'criminal possession of stolen property'; its base fifth-degree tier is value-independent, a straight Class A misdemeanor regardless of the property's worth. Florida's dedicated statute is built around trafficking in stolen property rather than passive receipt, which appears to fall back to the general theft statute instead. Virginia again uses its legal-fiction approach: receiving stolen property is deemed larceny outright, and prosecution can proceed even if the original thief was never convicted. Ohio removes a common defense entirely: it is not a defense that property wasn't actually stolen if it was explicitly represented to the defendant as stolen, meaning undercover sting property can still support a conviction. Illinois, like Ohio, has no standalone receiving-stolen-property statute; it is simply one of several listed ways to commit ordinary theft under the same general statute. Georgia builds an explicit good-faith carve-out directly into the offense definition: it's not a violation if the defendant's actual intent was to restore the property to its owner. North Carolina uses a single flat Class H felony regardless of the property's value, a notably simpler structure than the multi-tier value ladders most other states use. New Jersey's statute explicitly EXCLUDES motor vehicles from its own coverage, a genuine structural quirk not seen in any other state surveyed. Arizona, like Ohio and Illinois, has no standalone receiving-stolen-property statute; it is one of nine listed conduct paths within the same consolidated theft statute. Texas, like Ohio, Illinois, and Arizona, has no standalone receiving-stolen-property statute; it's one path within the same consolidated theft statute. Pennsylvania keeps this as its own separate statute with the same good-faith restore-to-owner carve-out found in several other states. Michigan uses the same combined dollar-and-prior-conviction cross-cutting grading as its own malicious destruction statute. Washington creates a rebuttable presumption of guilty knowledge from possessing bulk quantities of stolen access devices, pallets, or beverage crates. Massachusetts sets a distinctive $250 warrantless-arrest threshold separate from its $1,200 grading line. Tennessee's theft definition wasn't independently confirmed to explicitly list receiving stolen property as its own path; likely falls under the general control-without-consent language. Indiana folds this in through its definitions section, which explicitly includes 'possess' within 'exert control over property,' rather than a named receiving offense. Missouri explicitly names receiving stolen property as one of three listed ways to commit stealing directly in the statute's own text. Maryland explicitly lists knowingly possessing stolen property as one of the named ways to commit its consolidated theft offense. Wisconsin's theft statute wasn't independently confirmed to explicitly list receiving stolen property as its own named path; likely falls under the general unauthorized-control language. Colorado explicitly folds receiving stolen property directly into its consolidated theft statute's own text. Minnesota explicitly lists receiving stolen property as one of its 19 named theft conduct categories. South Carolina's middle value tier stays a misdemeanor even though it carries up to 3 years and a mandatory minimum $1,000 fine. Alabama treats possessing stolen property on two separate occasions within a year as prima facie evidence of the knowledge element. Louisiana adds a distinctive 72-hour self-reporting affirmative defense not framed this specifically in most other states surveyed. Kentucky grades this on the exact same value ladder and automatic-felony triggers as its sibling theft statute. Oregon folds this directly into its general theft framework, graded by the identical value ladder. Connecticut likewise folds this directly into its general larceny statute as one of its 18 named conduct categories. Utah protects compliant secondhand dealers and pawnbrokers from an automatic knowledge presumption if they followed recordkeeping requirements. Nevada treats mere possession of recently stolen property as prima facie evidence of knowledge, shifting a practical burden onto the defendant. Iowa folds this directly into its theft-definition statute as one of ten listed conduct categories. Arkansas allows an additional discretionary 5-year enhancement specifically for stolen nonferrous metal. Mississippi explicitly bars charging a defendant with both stealing and receiving the same stolen property. Kansas folds this directly into its general theft statute as one of five listed conduct types. Nebraska explicitly treats this as the same offense as theft by unlawful taking, not a separately titled crime. Idaho likewise folds this directly into its general theft-definition statute as one enumerated category. West Virginia explicitly prosecutes this as larceny itself, not a separately graded crime, even without the original thief being convicted. Hawaii likewise folds this directly into its general theft-definition statute as one named category. New Hampshire explicitly names this as one of the consolidated conduct types within its general theft statute. Maine keeps this as its own separately titled offense rather than folding it into the general theft definition. Montana explicitly folds this directly into its general theft statute as one of several listed conduct types. Rhode Island creates a genuine presumption of guilty knowledge from mere possession of stolen property alone. Delaware's receiving-stolen-property statute reaches felony status through either high value OR two prior convictions regardless of value. North Dakota folds receiving stolen property directly into its unified theft statute, but separately criminalizes organized trafficking in stolen goods as its own felony. Vermont's statute sets no independent penalty scale at all, tying the sentence entirely to the underlying theft's own value-based grading. DC's statute explicitly states it is no defense that the property was not actually stolen, so long as the defendant believed it was. Wyoming has no separate receiving-stolen-property statute, folding this conduct into the general theft law's own value-based grading instead. Alaska's 'theft by receiving' is likewise expressly consolidated with ordinary theft under the same value ladder rather than carrying its own separate penalty scale. New Mexico grades receiving stolen property on the same value ladder as theft and shoplifting, with any firearm under $2,500 carrying its own flat felony regardless of the general value tier. Puerto Rico uses a notably simple two-tier structure, a misdemeanor under $500 and a flat felony at or above that threshold.",
