    lastVerified: "2026-08",
    notes: "Abandonment or desertion of the child is itself prima facie evidence of willfulness under California's nonsupport statute, and a first offense is a straight misdemeanor while a subsequent offense after a formal parentage adjudication can be charged as a low-level felony. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.Covers two separate paths, a parent/guardian/custodian failing to support a child under 16 while financially able, and anyone bound by a court support order for a child under 18 who knowingly fails to pay. Florida's felony escalation is tied to a fourth or subsequent violation, or $5,000 or more owed for over a year, whichever comes first. Virginia's nonsupport statute lives in the Domestic Relations title rather than the criminal code, and requires the dependent to be in actual 'necessitous circumstances,' not just owed money. Ohio's felony escalation is tied to a distinctive time-based test, a prior conviction or 26 weeks of nonpayment within a 104-week window, rather than a flat dollar-arrears threshold. Illinois combines time AND dollar thresholds across a four-tier structure in its own dedicated Non-Support Punishment Act, and offers first-time offenders diversion to a state jobs program with the conviction expunged on completion. Georgia's felony escalation is tied specifically to leaving the state entirely, treating out-of-state flight as the key aggravating fact rather than the severity of the deprivation itself. North Carolina's parental-nonsupport path is explicitly a continuing offense with no statute of limitations running until the youngest child turns 18, but the offense stays misdemeanor-only with no felony tier at all. New Jersey's statute is unusually compact, a single flat fourth degree crime with no repeat-offense ladder or value-based escalation at all. Arizona's statute lives entirely in its domestic relations title, covers only child support (no spousal provision), and presumes minimum-wage earning capacity except for still-in-school teen parents. Texas is a flat state jail felony with no misdemeanor tier, and allows a conviction to rest on the uncorroborated testimony of a party to the offense. Pennsylvania has one of the lightest nonsupport penalties surveyed, only a summary offense at base, escalating to a misdemeanor solely for interstate evasion combined with a prior or 12+ months of arrears. Michigan runs a genuine two-track structure: informal nonsupport is a misdemeanor folded into the disorderly-persons statute, while violating an actual court support order is its own separate felony reaching 4 years. Washington, like Georgia and Pennsylvania, keeps this in its domestic relations title, and explicitly includes domestic partners alongside spouses. Massachusetts expressly bars courts from reducing current support owed while a nonsupport prosecution is pending. Tennessee's felony escalation triggers on either interstate evasion OR simply a prior nonsupport conviction followed by a new one, without requiring the person to have left the state. Indiana's statute is already a felony at the base tier with no misdemeanor tier, and includes a distinctive religious-treatment defense for spiritual healing through prayer. Missouri explicitly covers spousal nonsupport alongside parental nonsupport, and cuts off the inability-to-pay defense if the defendant purposely maintains that inability. Maryland pairs up to 3 years imprisonment with only a $100 fine cap, a notably mismatched penalty structure. Wisconsin explicitly covers GRANDCHILD support alongside spousal and child support, and grades entirely by a clean 120-consecutive-day time threshold rather than a dollar amount. Colorado's nonsupport statute is a single flat Class 5 felony with no misdemeanor tier or dollar-arrears threshold at all. Minnesota requires a prior attempt to serve a family-court contempt order before criminal prosecution can even begin, layering enforcement on top of an exhausted civil process. South Carolina's nonsupport offense stays a flat misdemeanor regardless of how long the nonpayment continues or how large the arrears grow. Alabama likewise stays a flat misdemeanor at any duration, and expressly extends to children born outside marriage once paternity is established. Louisiana stays a flat, notably light misdemeanor regardless of arrears, with a 30-day presumption-of-abandonment mechanism built in. Kentucky reaches felony status through any of three alternative triggers, a $2,500 arrearage, six months without payment, or destitute circumstances. Oregon starts at a genuinely severe base classification, Class C felony from the outset, unlike most states' misdemeanor base. Connecticut treats this primarily as a civil-enforcement matter, with wage withholding and contempt as the statute's main emphasis. Utah treats an offense committed while residing outside the state as independently felony-triggering, a distinctive out-of-state penalty. Nevada's felony threshold drops by half the arrearage amount, from $10,000 to $5,000, on a second violation. Iowa stays a flat class D felony with no misdemeanor tier, reached by either 1+ year of nonsupport or $5,000+ owed. Arkansas independently reaches felony status for merely leaving the state 30+ days while owing support, no arrears threshold required. Mississippi covers children up to age 21 rather than the more common 18-year cutoff used in most states surveyed. Kansas stays a flat severity level 10 felony, its lowest felony tier, with no misdemeanor path at all. Nebraska lets the defendant raise inability to pay as evidence negating intent, rather than requiring the state to prove ability to pay upfront. Idaho's base classification is a straight felony reaching up to 14 years, one of the harshest baseline treatments surveyed. West Virginia's felony threshold turns purely on a full 12 consecutive months of unpaid support, and covers only child support, not alimony. Hawaii requires repeated, willful failure, reflecting a stated preference for family court intervention over criminal prosecution. New Hampshire reaches felony status through any of three independent triggers, a full year of arrears, $10,000+ owed, or a prior conviction. Maine stays a single flat lowest-felony class with no misdemeanor tier, and probation may extend until the dependent turns 18. Montana stacks three independent escalation paths, standard, aggravated, and a further enhanced tier at 6+ months of arrears. Rhode Island frames the offense around risking public dependency rather than a fixed dollar or time threshold. Delaware repealed its criminal nonsupport statute in 1996 and now handles child support enforcement exclusively through civil contempt. North Dakota grades nonsupport entirely by the dollar amount unpaid, with an explicit affirmative defense for a disability that precludes gainful employment. Unlike several states surveyed that repealed criminal nonsupport entirely, Vermont keeps a live offense codified in its domestic relations title rather than the general criminal code. DC handles nonsupport as a criminal contempt remedy filed within the existing civil support case rather than a freestanding criminal statute. Wyoming's nonsupport statute escalates to a mandatory minimum term specifically on a repeat conviction or 60 or more days of unpaid court-ordered support. Alaska's version includes a religious-treatment exemption for recognized prayer practitioners and can trigger loss of recreational licenses as an added consequence. New Mexico's 'abandonment of dependent' has no misdemeanor base tier at all, a flat felony from the first violation. Puerto Rico splits nonsupport across two separately numbered articles by dependent type, child versus spouse or other family member, with neither carrying a felony tier.",
