    notes: "California's base resisting-arrest offense is a misdemeanor with its own built-in weapon-removal enhancements (non-firearm and firearm) layered on top, provable even for an unsuccessful attempt to take the officer's firearm. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.A short single-element statute (intentionally preventing an authorized arrest) with no separate weapon-based felony enhancement built into the section itself, unlike some states' resisting-arrest laws. Florida's violent-resisting statute is a straight third degree felony, with a separate lower-level non-violent resisting statute (§ 843.02) for passive noncompliance. Virginia folds resisting arrest into a broader obstruction-of-justice statute alongside intimidation and false statements to police, all sharing the same Class 1 misdemeanor baseline unless force or a threat ties to a drug, weapons, or violent-felony case. Ohio scales cleanly by harm across three tiers, from reckless/forcible resistance up to felony treatment specifically for a deadly weapon used or merely brandished. Illinois's base misdemeanor carries an unusual mandatory floor, 48 consecutive hours of imprisonment or 100 hours of community service that probation cannot reduce below, and requires a genuine underlying offense to have justified the arrest in the first place. Georgia carries a separate, specifically named felony for throwing bodily fluids at an officer, distinct from its general violent-resistance repeat-offense ladder. North Carolina requires the resistance to be the explicit PROXIMATE cause of any resulting injury, a causation element built directly into the statute's text. New Jersey grades using flight to resist as its own distinct middle tier, separate from and less severe than actual force or violence. Arizona explicitly names and defines a 'passive resistance' category, a nonviolent act intended merely to delay an arrest, as its own lower misdemeanor tier. Texas requires actual use of force, not merely threatening it, and explicitly eliminates the unlawful-arrest defense directly in the statute's text. Pennsylvania's statute is unusually narrow and single-tiered, with no separate deadly-weapon felony enhancement written into this section at all. Michigan is a genuine outlier: this offense is a felony at every tier, with no misdemeanor base at all, unlike nearly every other state surveyed. Washington is the opposite extreme: a single flat misdemeanor with no deadly-weapon felony enhancement written into the statute at all. Massachusetts's unlawful-arrest defense carve-out is conditional rather than absolute: it only removes the defense if the officer also wasn't using excessive force. Tennessee is notably lenient at the top: even a deadly weapon only elevates this to a Class A misdemeanor, with no felony tier at all. Indiana has an elaborately tiered vehicle-based escalation ladder, up to a Level 2 felony for vehicle operation causing death to a firefighter or officer. Missouri grades primarily by the severity of the underlying arrest itself, a felony-charge arrest automatically makes resisting a felony regardless of the resistance conduct. Maryland classifies this as a misdemeanor yet allows up to 3 years, a felony-level sentence attached to a misdemeanor label, one of several Maryland statutes surveyed with this pattern. Wisconsin separately grades false information or planted evidence that actually leads to an innocent person's conviction as its own Class H felony, distinct from the injury-based tiers. Colorado's version is a single flat Class 2 misdemeanor with no felony escalation or weapon-based enhancement at all. Minnesota separately covers obstructing firefighters and emergency medical personnel, and even threatening tax or licensing officials, under the identical penalty structure. South Carolina treats an assault on the officer as a wholly separate felony offense, not merely an enhanced version of simple resistance. Alabama's version is a single flat misdemeanor with no force-based or risk-based tiers at all. Louisiana's statute is unusually detailed on what counts as obstruction, down to crossing a police cordon via drone. Kentucky's version is a single flat Class A misdemeanor with no weapon-based felony enhancement at all. Oregon expressly excludes passive resistance, merely going limp, from qualifying as resisting at all. Connecticut equally covers interfering with a firefighter and escalates to a felony if the interference causes death or serious injury. Utah expressly protects merely recording an officer's plain-view public duties from counting as interference. Nevada grades this entirely by weapon type, a firearm reaches a full category higher than any other dangerous weapon. Iowa runs a separately graded, one-tier-higher track specifically for resisting a correctional officer while already in custody. Arkansas explicitly splits active resistance from merely passive refusal to submit, at two different misdemeanor tiers. Mississippi's version is a single flat misdemeanor with no felony tier or weapon enhancement written into this section at all. Kansas grades fleeing while armed with a firearm at severity level 5, several tiers above unarmed fleeing. Nebraska extends coverage to obstructing a police animal, and courts require an affirmative act, mere verbal refusal doesn't qualify. Idaho folds knowingly giving a false report to police into the same statute at the identical penalty. West Virginia independently reaches a 1-5 year felony specifically for attempting to disarm a covered officer. Hawaii's statute expressly clarifies it's no defense that the underlying arrest itself was unlawful. New Hampshire expressly states verbal protestations alone never constitute resisting arrest. Maine grades by the specific method used, merely refusing to stop is a lighter class than using force or creating injury risk. Montana expressly states it's no defense that the underlying arrest itself was unlawful. Rhode Island has no statute specifically titled resisting arrest; this general obstruction provision also covers firefighters. Delaware's resisting statute splits sharply by force level: passive noncompliance stays misdemeanor, but force or violence reaches a class G felony. North Dakota grades resisting by the severity of the underlying arrest itself, resisting a felony arrest is a class C felony versus misdemeanor for lesser arrests. Vermont's resisting statute stays a single flat offense with no weapon- or injury-based tiers. DC folds resisting directly into its assault-on-an-officer statute and expressly bars claiming the officer's authority was reasonably in doubt as a defense. Wyoming stacks three distinct offenses on the same officer interaction rather than grading a single crime by severity, escalating from a misdemeanor to a felony for causing injury or disarming the officer. Alaska keeps this a single flat class A misdemeanor with no felony tier at all, the same no-felony-tier structure Tennessee uses. New Mexico's version separately criminalizes willfully refusing to stop a vehicle for a marked police unit, layered onto the more common resisting and evading paths. Puerto Rico's version is an unusually broad nine-path misdemeanor that also reaches refusing to help stop a crime or answer a fiscal authority's questions, not just resisting arrest itself.",
