    notes: "⚠ PENDING ATTORNEY REVIEW (added 2026-08): This entry was AI-drafted and sourced against statutes but has not been reviewed by a licensed criminal defense attorney. See docs/attorney-review-charge-explanations.md. California treats forgery as a 'wobbler' (misdemeanor or felony at prosecutorial/court discretion), except Proposition 47 locked forgery of specified financial instruments valued at $950 or less into a straight misdemeanor absent disqualifying priors. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.New York's base forgery tier is a straight Class A misdemeanor; higher degrees escalate specifically for particular instrument categories like government documents, money, or securities, not covered by this base citation. Florida's forgery statute enumerates a specific list of covered document types (deeds, wills, bonds, insurance policies) rather than using a general standard. Virginia uses a general 'any writing' standard instead of an enumerated document list, carving out only checks and public records, which are covered by separate statutes. Ohio runs a value-based felony ladder for general forgery but keeps forged ID cards on a separate flat-misdemeanor track with its own mandatory minimum fine on a repeat conviction. Illinois explicitly criminalizes unlawfully using someone else's digital or electronic signature within its base forgery statute, and grades a forged Universal Product Code label and a forged academic degree at their own distinct, notably lower tiers. Georgia splits forgery across two independent axes, uttered-vs-possessed for ordinary writings and a $1,500 value line specifically for checks, rather than one unified scale. North Carolina grades purely by QUANTITY of counterfeit instruments (one vs. five or more) rather than by dollar value at all. New Jersey grades almost entirely by document TYPE (government documents, checks, IDs) rather than value, and separately criminalizes possessing forgery-making equipment as its own offense. Arizona uses a flat Class 4 felony for essentially any forgery, with its one enhancement tied to an unrelated crime, using a forged instrument to acquire a smuggling 'drop house.' Texas layers two independent grading systems onto the same statute: document type for government/commercial instruments, and dollar value when forgery is used to obtain property or a service. Pennsylvania grades purely by document type with no dollar-value component to the grading at all, a cleaner single-axis structure. Michigan uses a single flat felony tier reaching up to 14 years, with no value-based or document-type tiering at all. Washington is similarly flat (Class C felony) but adds a distinctive identity-theft venue rule letting prosecution occur wherever the victim resides. Massachusetts uses a single flat felony tier up to 10 years for a broad enumerated document list, with no value-based tiering at all. Tennessee grades by the theft-value ladder but with a distinctive floor: forgery can never be graded lower than a Class E felony regardless of value. Indiana explicitly separates forgery (requiring fraud intent) from a distinct counterfeiting offense needing only knowing conduct. Missouri's single flat felony tier covers destroying documents and falsifying objects like receipts alongside ordinary alteration. Maryland grades creating a counterfeit document as a felony but merely possessing one with fraudulent intent as a misdemeanor, despite the misdemeanor carrying a longer maximum term. Wisconsin splits forgery by document TYPE rather than value, with a distinctly lower misdemeanor tier for false antiquity claims, altered ID documents, and false craftsman labels. Colorado presumes intent to defraud whenever a forged document is presented specifically to a peace officer. Minnesota's most directly applicable citation is a check-specific forgery statute graded on the same value ladder as general theft. South Carolina grades forgery by dollar amount, with a genuinely low felony floor: any forged instrument under $10,000 is already felony-level. Alabama grades forgery by the type of instrument rather than dollar value, with checks and commercial instruments always reaching felony status. Louisiana is not graded by dollar value at all; forging and merely possessing a forged writing share the identical single felony tier. Kentucky's citation is framed as possession of a forged instrument rather than the act of forging itself. Oregon's first-degree felony tier specifically includes 15 or more retail receipts or product code labels, a modern anti-fraud addition. Connecticut folds physician prescription fraud directly into its general forgery statute rather than a separate drug-fraud offense. Utah is not graded by dollar value at all, and rejects the defense that signing one's own real name defeats a forgery charge. Nevada likewise applies one flat felony class regardless of the instrument's value, despite listing a wide range of instrument types. Iowa grades by the instrument's institutional significance, government documents reach felony while private contracts stay misdemeanor. Arkansas uses the identical institutional-significance split, government instruments reach first degree, private ones second degree. Mississippi grades forgery on the exact same value ladder as its own grand larceny statute. Kansas grades entirely by repeat-offense count rather than value, with a third conviction barring probation until a mandatory minimum is served. Nebraska's first-degree tier is reserved specifically for government and corporate financial instruments, not graded by value. Idaho likewise isn't graded by value or instrument type, a single flat felony with an unusually wide 1-to-14-year range. West Virginia has a modern demand-draft provision closing a specific check-fraud loophole involving 'no signature required' account drafts. Hawaii extends coverage to fraudulently encoding a check's magnetic ink character recognition numbers. New Hampshire grades by instrument type, with fake insurance certificates carrying their own distinct misdemeanor tier. Maine separately criminalizes causing someone to sign a document through deception, a trick-based path distinct from altering. Montana jumps from a modest county-jail misdemeanor to a 20-year felony ceiling based on scheme involvement or value over $1,500. Rhode Island isn't graded by value at all, and reaches an unusually specific list including lottery and transportation tickets. Delaware grades forgery by the TYPE of instrument forged, government securities reach the top tier, rather than by dollar value. North Dakota layers instrument-type and dollar-value paths into one statute; government obligations are automatically class B felony regardless of value. Vermont's forgery statute is a single flat felony tier with no value-based or instrument-type grading at all. DC grades forgery in three tiers combining instrument type and dollar value, with government instruments and securities reaching the top tier regardless of value. Wyoming's forgery statute carries a single felony tier up to 10 years, with a narrow misdemeanor carve-out for only two specific low-stakes document types. Alaska reserves its first degree tier narrowly for government-issued money, securities, and postage, with lower-value instruments falling to a lesser degree. New Mexico grades by dollar value like theft, but automatically elevates any forged real-property or testamentary instrument to a felony regardless of value. Puerto Rico's forgery statute is a flat fixed 3-year term with no value-based grading at all, structurally simpler than the value- or instrument-type-graded statutes found in most states surveyed.",
