    notes: "⚠ PENDING ATTORNEY REVIEW (added 2026-08): This entry was AI-drafted and sourced against statutes but has not been reviewed by a licensed criminal defense attorney. See docs/attorney-review-charge-explanations.md. California splits failure to appear by the underlying case: a felony case triggers § 1320.5 (up to 3 years, $10,000 fine), while a misdemeanor case or own-recognizance release falls under the separate misdemeanor statute, § 1320. Fresh Tier 2 sourcing project (2026-08): all 10 anchor states (CA, NY, FL, VA, OH, IL, GA, NC, NJ, AZ) plus expansion to the remaining 41 jurisdictions is ongoing.New York calls this offense 'bail jumping' and grades it by the underlying case's severity: first degree requires a Class A or B felony indictment and a failure to appear within 30 days of the required date, a Class D felony. Florida grades failure to appear purely by the underlying case type, felony versus misdemeanor, with no separate willfulness-mitigation framework spelled out in the statute. Virginia's statute explicitly requires courts to weigh mitigating circumstances like medical conditions and transportation issues, and states that an isolated prior nonappearance alone doesn't prove willfulness. Ohio grades purely by the underlying release type, felony versus misdemeanor, with most traffic misdemeanors carved out but DUI specifically kept in. Illinois is a genuine outlier: it has NO standalone criminal failure-to-appear offense at all, since the prior bail-jumping statute was left blank when the 2023 Pretrial Fairness Act abolished cash bail; non-appearance is now handled purely through a warrant or summons, not a separate charge. Georgia adds a distinctive third path: leaving the state to avoid appearing on any of 21 specifically enumerated misdemeanors is itself elevated to its own felony. North Carolina's felony tier extends beyond felony-charge releases to also cover release under a separate pretrial-conditions statute (G.S. 15A-536). New Jersey's top tier requires proof the defendant actually took flight or went into hiding, not just a willful no-show, a specific evasive-conduct element layered on top of willfulness. Arizona splits its misdemeanor tier further by HOW the appearance obligation arose, a mandatory court appearance is graded more seriously than a written promise or served notice. Texas grades entirely by the underlying charge type without requiring proof of flight or hiding for its felony tier, unlike New Jersey's and Pennsylvania's statutes. Pennsylvania requires the same flight-or-hiding element New Jersey's statute demands, and explicitly excludes obligations tied to probation or parole. Michigan makes any failure to appear on a felony-charge bond a straight felony with no misdemeanor tier at all, a notably harsh baseline. Washington grades this by four tiers that closely mirror the underlying charge's own classification, from Class A felony down to a flat misdemeanor. Massachusetts explicitly requires any sentence here to run consecutively to the sentence for the underlying offense. Tennessee is a genuine outlier: a single flat Class A misdemeanor regardless of the underlying charge's severity, with no felony tier at all. Indiana explicitly states the outcome of the original charge is irrelevant, a defendant can be convicted even if later acquitted of the underlying crime. Missouri grades in three tiers that closely mirror the underlying matter's own classification, down to a mere infraction for an underlying infraction. Maryland builds in a distinctive 30-day grace window: the crime isn't complete until the person fails to surrender within 30 days after bail is actually forfeited. Wisconsin has a genuinely distinctive third path: a witness required to post bail who fails to appear is its own separate Class I felony, independent of any underlying charge. Colorado attaches a genuinely severe mandatory minimum, 1 year for a felony case, consecutive to the underlying sentence, notably harsher than most other states. Minnesota grades felony-charge failure to appear as a proportional penalty, up to half the underlying crime's maximum sentence, with a 48-hour voluntary-surrender dismissal window. South Carolina's failure-to-appear statute lives in its bail-procedure title, with penalty scaled to the severity of the underlying charge. Alabama splits bail jumping into two degrees keyed entirely to whether the original charge was murder or a Class A/B felony. Louisiana appears to have no standalone criminal failure-to-appear offense, the same gap already found in Illinois in this project. Kentucky's first-degree tier applies specifically to a missed felony-charge appearance and builds in its own unavoidable-circumstances affirmative defense. Oregon splits by the severity of the underlying charge, misdemeanor-charge misses stay a misdemeanor, felony-charge misses reach felony. Connecticut separately treats a felony probationer skipping a violation hearing as equally serious as skipping the original trial. Utah grades entirely by the underlying charge's severity, felony, misdemeanor, or infraction. Nevada independently escalates even a misdemeanor charge to felony status if the person intentionally left the state. Iowa folds this penalty directly into its bail-conditions statute, graded purely by the original charge's classification. Arkansas mirrors the underlying charge's classification with unusual precision, down to matching misdemeanor sub-classes one notch up. Mississippi appears to have no standalone criminal failure-to-appear offense, the same pattern already found in Louisiana and Illinois in this project. Kansas builds a uniform 30-day grace window directly into the offense definition before the charge is even triggered. Nebraska builds a 3-day cure window directly into the offense definition before the charge is triggered. Idaho requires the defendant to have affirmatively fled or hidden to avoid apprehension before felony status applies. West Virginia appears to have no standalone criminal failure-to-appear offense, the same pattern already found in Louisiana, Mississippi, and Illinois. Hawaii's first-degree tier applies to any missed felony-charge appearance under one flat felony classification. New Hampshire mandates any sentence run consecutively to the underlying charge's own sentence, not concurrently. Maine establishes strict liability directly in the statute's text, the defendant's intent is irrelevant to guilt. Montana's statute doesn't apply to ordinary misdemeanor releases at all, unless a judge specifically released the person on recognizance. Rhode Island's general crimes title does not appear to contain a standalone failure-to-appear offense; likely handled via Title 12 or contempt. Delaware has no standalone bail-jumping statute; failure to appear is prosecuted as ordinary criminal contempt. North Dakota does have a standalone bail-jumping statute, grading by whether the underlying release was for a felony charge. Vermont does have a standalone bail-jumping statute, unlike several other states surveyed that rely only on general contempt authority. DC's failure-to-appear statute carries a full one-year mandatory minimum for a felony charge, and any sentence must run consecutively to whatever else is imposed. Wyoming has no separate failure-to-appear or bail-jumping offense; Title 6 contains no such provision and Title 7's bail chapter is purely procedural. Alaska covers criminal-negligence failure to appear with grading that tracks the underlying case's own felony or misdemeanor status. New Mexico covers willful failure to appear in a clean two-tier structure, a fourth degree felony for a felony-case release or a petty misdemeanor for a misdemeanor-case release. Puerto Rico likewise has no standalone failure-to-appear offense within its 309-article Código Penal, handling this instead through its Rules of Criminal Procedure's bail and release provisions.",
